// ===========================================================================
// SOFTWARE VALIDATION content — the deliverables for validating RITHI CRM as
// software used within a medical-device manufacturer's Quality Management
// System. Structured per the V-model and mapped to the applicable standards:
//
//   • Medical Devices Rules, 2017 (CDSCO) — Fifth Schedule, the QMS a
//     manufacturer must hold; aligned to ISO 13485
//   • ISO 13485:2016 §4.1.6 — validation of software used in the QMS
//   • ISO/TR 80002-2:2017 — validation of software for medical-device QMS
//   • GAMP 5 (2nd ed.) — categories & life-cycle
//   • Computer Software Assurance (CSA) — risk-based, critical-thinking effort
//   • ISO 14971:2019 — risk management (applied to the software's QMS use)
//   • Data integrity: ALCOA+
//
// NOT 21 CFR. Part 820 and Part 11 are FDA's, and this operation is regulated
// by CDSCO. The CONTROLS Part 11 describes — an attributable, time-stamped
// trail users cannot alter, unique identities, authority checks — are kept:
// they are how record control under ISO 13485 §4.2.5 and the Fifth Schedule is
// evidenced. It is the citation that has gone, not the control.
// (Removed 2026-09-05 at the user's direction. India has no dedicated
// electronic-records rule for medical devices; electronic records and
// signatures take their legal standing from the Information Technology Act,
// 2000. Clause numbers here are the author's mapping and are for RA/QA to
// confirm against the current text of each standard.)
//
// This is a DRAFT package authored to be REVIEWED, APPROVED and EXECUTED by the
// company's QA/Validation function. It does not itself assert a validated state.
// ===========================================================================

export const VAL_META = {
  system: 'RITHI CRM — Field Service Module',
  owner: 'Air Liquide Medical Systems (ALMS) — Service / Quality',
  docId: 'VAL-RITHI-CRM',
  packageVersion: '3.1 (DRAFT)',
  status: 'DRAFT — pending QA review & approval',
  revision:
    'Rev 1.1 extends the package to the spare-stock control work: partial issue of spares (URS-021), acknowledged receipt per delivery (URS-022), reconciliation of consumption — booking, amendment and voiding (URS-023), the cap holding consumption within hand stock (URS-024), call re-opening (URS-025) and preventive-maintenance due-month scheduling (URS-026), with the corresponding FRS-026..032, design, risks R-13..R-15, FMEA FM-15/FM-16 and tests OQ-15..OQ-19 and PQ-04. Rev 1.2 adds refurbished spares (URS-027) and dispatch performance (URS-028), with FRS-033/034, R-16, FM-17 and OQ-20; hand stock is now derived from the spare ISSUE rather than the request, so a recycled part is held under its own code. Rev 1.3 adds the document library — service manuals presented on the call for the product they cover (URS-029) and controlled QMS documents (URS-030) — with FRS-035/036, R-17, FM-18 and OQ-21. Rev 1.4 covers the work asked for by the Reporting Managers and the correction that followed it: finding a machine or a customer’s machines (URS-031), allotment and bulk re-allotment of calls (URS-032), grouping a register by up to three columns (URS-033), raising a record for an engineer in one’s own team (URS-034), and correcting the engineer on a spare order before it is issued — never after — with a log of every such change (URS-035); with FRS-037..041, design for register reading, search and acting-for-a-team-member, risks R-18..R-20, FMEA FM-19/FM-20 and tests OQ-22..OQ-25. It also adds reliability and consumption analysis (URS-036, FRS-042, OQ-29) — failure rate per machine in the field, and spare use by cover and by region — and fills three requirements that had carried no test at all (OQ-26..28: master data, warranty and contract cover, customer feedback). Rev 1.5 covers the migration of the superseded system’s record and what it exposed: migrated data must stay DISTINGUISHABLE from the system’s own, and a figure derived from both must report the split (URS-037); a stock period may be closed, provably without changing a balance (URS-038); identifiers continue across a migration rather than being re-issued (URS-039); a bulk load alters only what its file carries (URS-040); migrated stock is opened only against an active user (URS-041); and a register returns within a working time on production volume (URS-042) — with FRS-043..048, design for migrated data, bulk loading and response time, risks R-21..R-25, FMEA FM-21..FM-23, tests OQ-30..34 and PQ-05, four further data-migration checks (DM7..DM10) and four configuration items, among them the rule that a schema bundle must carry the latest definition of everything it defines. Rev 1.6 adds decision support (URS-043, FRS-049, R-27, OQ-36) — a suggested Standard Complaint, drawn from the register, stating its grounds, never written without a person choosing it — and, found while testing it, the control that a VIEW must not defeat the policies beneath it (FRS-050, R-26, OQ-35): `calls` had lost `security_invoker` when 0057 rebuilt it, so every signed-in user could read every call, and pending_calls and call_state inherited that reach despite carrying the setting themselves. Rev 1.5 also restates FRS-021: a BULK write to a quality table is recorded as one attributable event rather than a row per record (0103), because an audit trail answers what a person changed and a data load is answered by the migration checks — the trail keeps who loaded what, how much and when. Rev 1.7 changes the package’s REGULATORY BASIS and one of its controls, both at the user’s direction (2026-09-05). 21 CFR is removed: Part 820 and Part 11 are FDA’s, and this operation is regulated by CDSCO under the Medical Devices Rules, 2017, whose Fifth Schedule sets the QMS and is aligned to ISO 13485. Every Part 11 / Part 820 citation is re-cited to ISO 13485 or the Fifth Schedule, and the Part 11 appendix is withdrawn. The CONTROLS those clauses describe are kept — an attributable, time-stamped trail users cannot alter, unique identities, authority checks — because they are how record control is evidenced; it is the citation that has gone. India has no dedicated electronic-records rule for medical devices; electronic records and signatures take their standing from the Information Technology Act, 2000. AND THE DATABASE-ENFORCED TRAIL IS SWITCHED OFF (0112): record_audit no longer records anything. Its table is retained and readable, holding what it captured while it ran. FRS-021 now describes ONE trail and states its limits — client-written, so bypassable by a direct API call, and purged on the retention window — and R-14 is raised from Low to Medium residual because the independent before/after image of an amended consumption entry is gone. The clause mappings in this revision are the author’s and are for RA/QA to confirm against the current text of each standard and of MDR-2017. Rev 1.8 splits the registrant of a call in two (URS-044, FRS-051, R-28, FM-24, OQ-37): `created_by` is the Hotline DESK the call is filed to — the Hotline engineer is the only person trained on the vigilance questions — and `actual_created_by` is the individual who registered it, taken from the authenticated session and discarded if a caller supplies one. The two DIFFERING is the finding; where neither can be determined, because the record was bulk-loaded from the superseded system, both are empty and the record says so. Rev 1.8 also introduces the package’s first NON-AUDITABLE REQUIREMENT (NAR-001, Audit Mode): an administrator-only switch requested by the system owner, whose governing rules have not yet been supplied and on which no application behaviour is conditioned. It is classified by PROVENANCE — not derived from a regulatory clause and not offered as evidence against one — and every change of the switch is recorded with its reason in a table the API cannot write to and the retention purge does not touch (OQ-38). Any future rule that would alter, conceal or suppress a quality record is outside that classification and is to be raised as an auditable requirement with its own risk assessment. Rev 1.9 records four requirements from the servicing work of 2026-09-08/09. URS-045 makes CONTROLLED VOCABULARY a requirement rather than a habit: where a field must match a list the value is chosen and never typed, and no keystroke commits one — FRS-052 replaces every native dropdown in the application with a pick list that filters on typing and commits only on a click or Enter, and FRS-053 removes the free-text fallback from the Standard Complaint on all five screens that ask for it. The control matters because counting, filtering and repeat-failure detection all match on the stored value: a hand-entered variant is a record no analysis will find. URS-046/FRS-054 bring repeat-failure detection onto the rule the procedure states — counting the call under review, within a month, on the same equipment OR the same part in the same machine — with the window and threshold maintainable by an administrator and the rule shown beside the verdict, because judgements recorded under the previous six-month rule remain in the register and are deliberately not re-based. A machine that cannot be identified now reports \u201Ccannot tell\u201D rather than \u201Cno repeat failure\u201D, that being the direction which would otherwise talk somebody out of raising a field failure report. URS-047/FRS-055 make a spare sent to a call accountable against it; URS-048/FRS-056 carry a maintenance contract into its successor without re-keying and without carrying its prices, with cover continuous by construction. Rev 1.9 also adds docs/DATABASE_SCHEMA.md as a configuration item: it is GENERATED from a database built out of the migrations rather than written, because a schema description that is wrong is worse than none. Rev 2.0 covers the Indoor Service register (2026-09-09), which brings a clause into scope that a field-only reading of the servicing provision does not reach: once the organisation takes a customer’s device onto its own premises it holds that person’s property. URS-049 requires a register that identifies the equipment, states WHOSE it is and records the condition it arrived in — with the ownership held on an axis SEPARATE from the work being done, so a demonstration unit does not become customer property because it came in for repair, and with no service call required, a demonstration unit having none. URS-050 requires decontamination to a work instruction AND ITS REVISION before the equipment is worked on, and makes it a precondition rather than a note: the recovery of parts is REFUSED by the database while it is outstanding, the only control in the module that blocks, because it protects the person doing the work and not the product. URS-051 makes the quality check its own record and its own PERMISSION, separable from the authority to do the work so the two may be different people — enforced in the database, so somebody holding every other authority is still refused; equipment with a failed check, and work of a kind that requires a check and has none, cannot leave. Whether the two must be different people is deliberately NOT enforced, the procedure being silent: both identities are recorded and the screen states when they coincide, which is the auditable form of an unsettled rule. URS-052 makes condemnation an authorised act with a permission of its own, granted to the administrator alone on apply, and requires recovered parts to be recorded with their condition and to enter NO stock balance — a recovered part under its ordinary code cannot afterwards be told from a new one. FRS-057..060, risks R-29..R-32 and tests OQ-43..46 follow. The requirement to carry a service call across a transfer between departments is NOT met and is stated as outstanding; the register is built so that it stays closable — the link to the originating call is present and nullable, and no view over the call tables was rebuilt. Rev 2.1 records the servicing work of 2026-09-11 and, unusually, one requirement written from a DEFECT FOUND IN USE rather than from a clause. URS-053 makes the serial number mandatory from the request onward: a call was raised as REQID-Product-NA, naming a product but no unit, and its party, location, cover, warranty and contract all had to be corrected by hand against Product Database afterwards — a record that cannot be traced to the device serviced is not a servicing record. Where the serial is absent because the machine is not on the master, the field says so rather than inviting itself to be skipped, the master being the thing to fix. URS-054 requires the record of parts fitted to be COMPLETE, and is the defect: the visit report wrote only the consumption lines committed with an explicit Add, so a part entered and visible on screen was discarded on save — one line short on every report that ended that way, silently, and wrong twice over because hand stock is DERIVED from consumption, leaving the engineer’s balance overstated by the part that went into the machine. The answer \u201CAdd Consumption?\u201D is now Yes or None Consumed, neither of which is a skip, and Yes requires a line. The rule is held as a function exercised with real inputs rather than as a condition inside a form, after a check written over the form’s source passed against code that had been disabled. URS-055 adds the review of a closed call’s report, separate from the daily coding of the failure and asking a different question — does this report close this call — with the reviewer taken from the authenticated session, the authority to review held as its own permission, and the two corrections a reviewer needs: booking a part that was fitted and not recorded, and returning the call to open. URS-056 makes explicit a control the system already depended on and did not state: an access role always has a defined permission set. A role may now be added from the application, and it can be added only by derivation, because has_perm() reads an empty permission array as unconfigured and falls back to the engineer defaults — so a role holding \u201Cnothing\u201D would confer an engineer’s write authority on everyone placed on it, and would show as holding nothing to anyone reviewing it. FRS-061..065, risks R-33..R-35, FMEA FM-25/FM-26 and tests OQ-47..50 follow. R-34 and FM-25 are recorded with a HIGH initial occurrence rather than a nominal one: this failure was not hypothesised, it was reported from use. Rev 2.2 (2026-09-12) adds ONE requirement, URS-057, from the system owner’s ask that users be able to save their signatures. It is written as a CONFIDENTIALITY requirement rather than a convenience, because a mark is worth exactly what its exclusivity is worth: a signature a second person can obtain is one they can place on any record, and a signature a second person can SET is one nobody signed. The stored signature is therefore readable and writable by its owner ALONE — by no manager and, deliberately, by no administrator — and it is reproduced on a document only where the signature block names the person producing that document; every other block prints empty for signature by hand, as the controlled forms are completed today. It is held in a table of its own rather than on the profile because Row-Level Security grants by ROW, so a policy admitting a person to save a signature on their profile row would admit them to rewrite the role and permissions on it. FRS-066..068, risk R-36, FMEA FM-27 and test OQ-51 follow. Two findings are recorded because they were produced by testing rather than reasoning and both would have shipped: a DELETE policy admitting an administrator DOES NOT WORK — PostgreSQL applies the SELECT policy to a DELETE that must locate its row, so the statement succeeded and affected nothing — and the administrative functions are definer FUNCTIONS rather than views, because a definer view over Row-Level-Security-protected tables reads as its owner and defeats the policies beneath it, a fault this system shipped three times and now refuses automatically. This revision states a LIMITATION plainly: the signature is reproduced from a stored image at the time a document is produced. It is not a cryptographic signature, it binds nothing to the document’s content, and it is not offered as evidence of an electronic signature under the Information Technology Act, 2000. Where assent must be evidenced, the attributable audit trail and the authority checks remain the controls; this is the reproduction of a handwritten mark on a printed form, and the printed form remains the record. Rev 2.3 (2026-09-12) records the controlled form and two requirements about attribution. The Word copy of the Field Failure Report now reproduces R-SER-03 Rev 02 ITSELF rather than a tidier document carrying the same fields — a controlled form held in a different shape is a different form, however good it looks — and a printable HTML page renders the same form from the same definition, so the two cannot drift. URS-058 requires a judgement on a quality record to name the person who made it, taken from the authenticated session at the moment the judgement is completed: the review screen stamped a name only when somebody pressed Save, while the same review could be completed by an automatic save or in bulk, so reports were being raised naming \u201CDaily Call Review\u201D — a screen, not a person. FRS-069 moves the stamp into the database on every path, and records a fault found only by exercising it: PostgreSQL computes a GENERATED column AFTER the BEFORE triggers run, so the trigger’s first version read review2_done as NULL and stamped nothing, silently, everywhere. URS-059 requires every amendment to a field failure report to be recorded with its before and after values, its author and its time — FRS-070 does this in the database rather than in the application, because the application’s own trail is client-written (FRS-021) and sees nothing of an edit made through the API; the log has no insert, update or delete policy at all. FRS-066..070, risks R-36..R-38, FMEA FM-27..FM-29 and tests OQ-51..53 follow. Rev 2.4 (2026-09-13) records five requirements, four of them written from FAULTS FOUND WHILE LOADING THE SUPERSEDED SYSTEM’S OWN EXPORTS rather than from a clause, and each established by MEASUREMENT against the data rather than by reading the schema. URS-060 requires a record to be keyed on the whole of what identifies it: a device is its MODEL together with its SERIAL, serials being repeated across models, and one document covering several devices gives each device its own record. Three registers were keyed on less, and a key narrower than the identity does not fail loudly — it silently replaces one record with another. The field failure register lost twelve machines that way and the recovered-warranty register three hundred and forty-two, on loads that reported success; the rule was already written in the codebase, with the incident that produced it, and these registers contradicted it. URS-061 requires a value the system cannot determine to be left EMPTY and the record kept, rather than filled with something available but untrue or discarded for want of it — completing an ownership transfer’s previous owner from a machine master that had already caught up produced \u201Cfrom Apollo to Apollo\u201D and halted 2,985 rows of 4,327. URS-062 requires every register loadable from a file to carry a natural key the data interface can INFER, so a corrected export updates rather than arrives twice; the check that verifies this against a real database refused a partial index written during this revision, a partial index being no more inferable than an expression one. URS-063 requires a role described as read-only to hold no authority that writes, and a role COPIED from another not to track it thereafter: an authority granted deliberately on one role had been crossing to another on every re-application of configuration, and what it conferred — raising a field failure report that can never be deleted — was established by exercising it. It also requires such a holding to be reported as a matter for REVIEW rather than as a defect, because a status row that reads NO on a deliberate grant sends somebody to re-apply configuration that cannot remove it; that row had done exactly that, twice. URS-064 requires that no part of a credential can be recovered from a log: masking the value is not sufficient, because a subsystem reports the PIECE it failed on, and a password containing an unencoded \u201C@\u201D made the database client report a host built from a suffix of it — a string matching neither the password nor the URL, which reached a public build log twice while the platform’s own masking displayed the secret as masked. The operation is now refused before the client is reached. THE EXPOSURE ALREADY MADE IS NOT UNDONE BY THIS: the credential requires rotation, and that is recorded as outstanding. FRS-072..076, risks R-39..R-43, FMEA FM-30..FM-32 and tests OQ-54..57 follow. Rev 2.5 (2026-09-15) is the first revision written by MEASURING THE PACKAGE AGAINST THE APPLICATION rather than by recording new work, at the user’s direction: \u201CScan Page by Page, Module by Module - List down all Actions, Views, Check if the Requirements are up to Date\u201D. Two GENERATED reports now do that on every run — docs/MODULE_INVENTORY.md (all 54 screens: the key that opens each, what an administrator may GRANT on it, what the screen itself tests, and what it reads) and docs/REQUIREMENT_COVERAGE.md (which screens and actions the package does not MENTION). Both state what they cannot prove: requirements are prose, one can cover a screen without naming it, and no scan sees that — so every line is a question for a human rather than a defect, a coverage report that overstates being one that gets argued with instead of acted on. THE MEASUREMENT FOUND THREE SCREENS AND EIGHT ACTIONS NAMED NOWHERE IN THE PACKAGE. URS-065/FRS-077 (OQ-58) cover the recovery of visit history from the superseded system: read, resolve, and only then write, with nothing written until the operator has SEEN what each row resolved to — a visit attached to the wrong call is a false record of what was done to a device, which is worse than a gap that is visible as a gap. URS-066/FRS-078 (OQ-59) cover the queue of requests awaiting registration and require each to leave it by one of three stated routes, a request nobody can see being indistinguishable from one nobody made. URS-067/FRS-079 (OQ-60) state what the call permissions had always done and the package had never said: authority over a call in progress is held SECTION BY SECTION — the complaint, the customer and device, the vigilance answers, the contact details — with re-allocation and cancellation each their own right, because correcting a telephone number and re-answering whether a patient was harmed are not the same act. The Tracker is recorded as NAR-002, a non-auditable requirement: it is a shared activity list, nothing in the servicing process reads it, and should anything a procedure relies on ever be kept there that use is outside the classification — a shared list with no attribution per field and no amendment history cannot carry a record anybody must rely on. FOUR REQUIREMENTS THAT CARRIED NO TEST AT ALL NOW HAVE ONE: FRS-022 record integrity (OQ-61, with supabase/tests/retention_test.sql written for it), FRS-014 stock visibility scope (OQ-62), FRS-071 the year-by-year field failure load (OQ-63) and FRS-023 backup and restore (PQ-06). Every URS and every FRS in the package now traces to at least one test. AND A TEST PROTOCOL MAY NOW NAME THE COMMAND THAT RUNS IT (`auto`), so the package can state how many of its own tests are EXECUTED rather than read — a protocol nobody runs is a document, not a test. That claim is itself checked, and it was already wrong once on the run that introduced it: OQ-61 named a retention suite before the suite existed. The suite was written rather than the claim dropped, FRS-022 being high risk. THREE FALSE FINDINGS PRODUCED BY THE NEW REPORTS were caught before any of them became work, and are recorded because the pattern matters more than the instances: eight screens reported as having no component (all three causes were the script — `export const` components, parameterised routes, and `Navigate` matching the element pattern first); `/exports` reported as absent from the menu when it is the parent key whose five reports are listed instead; and three screens reported as ungrantable when a page’s own key is granted by its PAGE ENTRY rather than by its actions list. A report that overstates is the failure mode of this kind of measurement, and it is the reason both files say so at the top. Rev 3.0 (2026-09-30) READ EVERY PAGE, ONE ACTION AT A TIME, at the user’s direction (“Read every page one by one, list all actions, and take every single capability and update the requirements document”): docs/CAPABILITY_INVENTORY.md lists 1,072 actions across every screen, each with the line it was read from and what guards it — 474 had no requirement stating them and 309 were stated only in part. The package gained 87 URS, 121 FRS, 126 tests, 61 risks, 61 FMEA rows and 58 defects, after folding duplicates, and 27 existing statements the reading proved false were corrected. Every URS traces to an FRS and a test, and every FRS to a test; no screen and no action is unnamed. Four data flows are defined with the package and drawn from it. 53 defects were open, and the tests that depend on them say they are expected to fail until each is fixed. Rev 3.1 (2026-10-02) brings the package level with what shipped after Rev 3.0 (v0.10.8 to v0.10.21) and records what that work found. NEW SCREENS AND ACTIONS, each with its requirement and test: Part Search, read-only for every role (URS-167, FRS-217, OQ-211); the header search across ten registers, each hit opening its own record (URS-168, FRS-218, OQ-212, R-105); the HSN Code on the Part Master and its one-time fill from the descriptions (FRS-219, OQ-213); My Profile in tabs and My Team with current and ex employees (FRS-216.5–.6, OQ-214); Technical / Service Notes loaded in bulk on the Drive link, carrying Drive’s dates beside RITHI’s own, naming several products, and offered on a call (FRS-035, OQ-215) — the bulk load had shipped stated nowhere. WHAT THE WORK FOUND, as defects: a part rename never moved stock adjustments (D-076, fixed); the right to rename a part was not enough to rename one on another engineer’s spare request or on a material return, which a one-time correction on the live project exposed by renaming 12 of 29 parts and silently leaving 17 (D-077, fixed); with no signed-in user the spare-line parts rule reads NULL and lets a change through — not reachable from the application, recorded and left open as a decision rather than a fix (D-078); the migration run discarded what a migration said, which is why the 17 went unnoticed until a read-only probe (D-079, fixed — the run now prints every notice); the Reports tab bar never showed the open tab (D-080, fixed). R-106 and FM-94 record the risk of a one-time correction applied to the live record and the controls now around it: the same rename a person uses, idempotent, its notices printed, and a probe read before and after. The package now holds 168 URS, 219 FRS, 223 tests (56 executed by a named command), 106 risks, 94 FMEA rows and 80 defects, 50 of them open.',
  standards: [
    'Medical Devices Rules, 2017 (CDSCO) — Fifth Schedule (QMS)',
    'ISO 13485:2016 §4.1.6 (validation of QMS software)',
    'ISO/TR 80002-2:2017',
    'GAMP 5 (2nd edition)',
    'Computer Software Assurance (CSA) — risk-based validation effort',
    'ISO 14971:2019 (risk management)',
    'Information Technology Act, 2000 (legal standing of electronic records)',
  ],
  gampCategory:
    'Predominantly GAMP Category 5 (custom application) running on Category 1/4 infrastructure & configured platform services (Supabase/Postgres, GitHub Pages).',
  intendedUse:
    'RITHI CRM records and manages field-service operations for medical devices: service/complaint-adjacent call registration, engineer visit reports, spare-part requests and approvals, stock movements, preventive-maintenance scheduling, warranty/contract cover and customer feedback. It is used within the QMS to create and retain quality records; it is NOT medical-device software and does not perform a medical-device function.',
};

